65:**Ids are never reused.** The file stores a `nextId` counter next to the todos. If ids were derived from the largest existing id, deleting todo #5 and adding a new one would give the new todo #5, and a `complete 5` typed from memory would hit the wrong item. A bare JSON array of todos is also accepted when reading (for example a file written by hand); its next id is the largest id plus one, and the next write converts it to the object format. If a hand-edited `nextId` is not above every existing id, it is raised so ids stay unique.
67:**Invalid files are never overwritten.** If `todos.json` is not valid JSON, or a todo is missing a field or has the wrong type, every command exits 1 with the file path, the todo's index and the field at fault. The file is left as it was. A missing or empty file is treated as an empty list, and a leading UTF-8 byte order mark is ignored. Unknown fields on a todo are preserved when the file is rewritten.
69:**Writes are atomic.** The new content goes to a temporary file in the same directory, is flushed to disk, then renamed over `todos.json`. A crash mid-write leaves the old file intact, and `list` never sees a half-written file, which is why `list` does not take the lock.
71:**Concurrent commands do not lose updates.** `add`, `complete` and `delete` read, modify and write the file while holding `todos.json.lock`, created with an exclusive open. Other invocations retry every 25 ms and give up after 5 seconds with a message naming the lock file. The lock holds the owner's pid: if that process no longer exists, or the lock is more than 30 seconds old, it is treated as abandoned and taken over, so a crashed command does not block the list.
